B.b_=new A.a1(B.qn,["Every component in this kit is copied into your app, so the source is yours to read, edit and extend. The registry defines each component once: its widget file, its style file, its theme overrides and a generated manifest the CLI installs from.","There is exactly one implementation per component. No variant wrappers, no per-style subclasses and no Material widgets under the hood; the components are built from the kit primitives and the active theme tokens.","Source you own","A component arrives as a folder under your UI directory. The theme file inside it is user-owned: CLI updates never overwrite your overrides, so you can change any token-driven value without forking the registry.","Open source: ","the component files are plain Flutter widgets you can diff, review and commit.","Predictable interfaces: ","one constructor plus enums, so examples and AI tools can learn the pattern once.","Generated docs: ","the {count} reference pages are built from the registry manifest, never hand-written.","One command install","Add a component with ",". The CLI writes the component, its shared dependencies and a user-owned theme file, and records the exact hashes in a lock file so updates stay reviewable.","Presets included","The kit ships {presets} theme presets across {modes} modes. A preset is data: colour tokens, radius and fonts, resolved through the same theme layer the components read at build time.","Try them on the Themes page, or read the Theming page to see how a preset maps onto the token names.","Use the components two ways, from the same source: depend on the published package and update via semver, or copy the sources into your project and own them. Pick a tab \u2014 the steps below walk the own-the-code flow.","Create a Flutter project","Install the layer core","Add components","Choose a preset","Verify","After installation, your project has the registry layer core (foundation + theme) and the components you added. Run the app to see the components in action.","Layer core: ","foundation, theme and primitives install first; components depend on them.","Lock file: ","dashcn.lock v2 records the exact hashes so updates stay reviewable.","User-owned themes: ","*_theme.dart files are never overwritten by CLI updates.","Start from a template: "," \u2014 one command scaffolds a runnable app from flows and blocks.","Own-the-code apps stay on the package path too: run "," later to turn a package import into owned sources.","Every component ships twice from the same source: as the published dashcn package and as copyable files the CLI installs into your app. Both render the same widgets from the same theme tokens; they differ only in who owns the bytes.","Which one?","Choose the package when you want semver updates and lean imports. Choose own-the-code when you want to read, edit and commit every line.","Per-component imports keep package-mode builds lean; only the components you import are compiled in.","How eject works","Started on the package and outgrew it? "," copies that component (plus its closure) into your app and rewrites the app imports to match. "," also ejects its transitive component dependencies.","How updates differ","Package: ","update with the package manager; breaking changes arrive as new major versions.","Own the code: ","run dashcn update. Unchanged files are overwritten, locally edited files are reported and skipped, and user-owned *_theme.dart files are never touched.","The lock file","dashcn.lock (v2) records the registry reference, the install root, the theme selection and \u2014 per component \u2014 whether it is a package dependency or owned sources. .dashcn/config.json holds the install path and registry source. Commit both.","dashcn is widgets-only: no Material widgets under the hood, no MaterialApp required. In a mixed app the two sets coexist behind import prefixes.","The clashing names","Theme, ThemeData, Card, Chip, Dialog, Slider, Switch, Checkbox, Tooltip, showDialog and showMenu exist in both libraries. Import one side with a prefix so both stay usable.","A mixed example","With the prefix, both sets stay usable in one tree: Material scaffolding outside, dashcn components inside.","Theming the island","Wrap the dashcn subtree in its Theme so tokens resolve below the boundary.","DashcnApp vs MaterialApp","One root owns the app: use DashcnApp for a full dashcn app, MaterialApp for a Material app. Never nest one root inside the other.","Touch targets","On phones dashcn grows desktop-density controls with transparent hit padding (iOS 44, Android 48) without changing the painted visuals. Dense rows and joined groups can opt out per app with ThemeData(touchTargets: TouchTargets.shrinkWrap)."],t.w)
